india has reported a staggering  400 000 new coronavirus infections in  just  24 hours adding to a week-long streak of  world  record breaking numbers to battle the  devastating second wave  saturday should have marked the start of  a vaccine rollout for hundreds of  millions of over eight teens in india  but most states are not taking part  because there are not  nearly enough vaccines to go around  these are the lucky ones waiting in line  to be vaccinated  and eventually being given their jabs  but plans to begin vaccinating all  adults are in disarray  many states simply don't have any doses  to give out  we are requesting the people of  karnataka especially those who are  above 18 years up to 44 years  to refrain from going to the hospitals  thinking that you may be vaccinated  the company's manufacturing vaccines  will not be able to deliver the doses on  the first of may  so the vaccination drive in madhya  pradesh will not begin  we are completely prepared our  healthcare workers are ready  but we do not have the vaccines  only around 1 in 10 of india's vast  population  has had even a first jab the lack of  doses made worse by  administrative inefficiencies  oxygen and medicines are also in short  supply  pharmacies say there has been panic  buying and hoarding  and they are restricting supplies only  giving out oxygen cylinders to people  who can prove they really need them  emergency care facilities are being set  up  sports halls religious sites and  catering facilities  are being turned into makeshift kovid 19  isolation wards  and the awful task of dealing with the  dead goes on  in the capital delhi volunteers put  themselves at risk  collecting the bodies of those who've  died  two days back i picked up on my own  uncle and he died because we couldn't  supply him oxygen i was working in the  mortuary van  i couldn't pick up the call and the  moment i was able to receive the call he  was already passed away  so it's it's really tough for us  an international relief effort is  underway  to help india fight its devastating  second wave  but deaths and infections are still  rising remorselessly  and with not enough vaccines to go round  for more we're joined now by dw's  nemisia jaiswal hello to you namisha  so india was supposed to expand its  vaccination program today but as we  heard in the report there's simply  not enough vaccine doses to go around  so what will today's rollout look like  well mariana today's rollout is  continuing  but it will in a way highlight india's  systemic issues  of inequality because while the  government has technically allowed  everybody above 18 to be vaccinated  many states including delhi and  maharashtra  two of the worst hit states have said we  do not have enough doses  please do not come out and queue outside  vaccination centers  we will let you know when we have doses  hopefully in a few days  now the indian government has allowed  states to buy directly from  manufacturers  which may well be behind some of these  shortages but in addition  those few people who are getting  vaccinated today  are getting vaccinated at private  hospitals because the government has  also allowed manufacturers  to sell to private hospitals but at a  higher price so those of us getting  vaccinated today are those of us  who can afford to pay for the vaccine  and this once again  will push behind those who cannot afford  it  namisha we do know that india is a major  producer of vaccines and yet it's still  struggling to ramp up vaccine production  for its own population  what is the reason for that  well mariana critics analyze and many  health experts have pointed out that the  indian government  while being aware of the fact that india  is a production hub  may have been too laid back in placing  its orders  some of the most some of the biggest  orders actually only went out in january  and this caused a funding crunch for  these manufacturers  the the serum institute of india the  manufacturer that's rolling out the  oxford vaccine  came out and made a statement saying we  do not have enough funding right now  to ramp up production since then of  course the indian government has  raised orders but they will still need  time to ramp up production  there is also criticism of the us for  not transferring raw material  and reserving it for domestic use that  policy has also been revised  it's also important to note that the  indian government does not admit  to a shortage of production capacity  when states have said we do not have  enough vaccines  the national government has said you  actually do so there is also a problem  of the lack of acknowledgement of the  problem at hand  it's a very difficult situation indeed  in india dw's correspondent  misha jaiswal thank you so much for your  reporting
 

